   "text": "I read the structure region by region and assembled the SMILES below. I did not run it through a parser.\n\n**Fragments**\n- **Left aryl group:** a 4-cyano-3,5-dimethylphenyl ring joined through an ether oxygen.\n- **Cyclobutane:** a four-membered ring with carbons in the order C(OAr), C(CH3)2, C(NH), C(CH3)2. The aryl ether and the amide nitrogen sit on opposite carbons of the ring.\n- **Amide and phenyl:** the NH is acylated by a para-substituted benzoyl group, whose ring carries the piperidine nitrogen.\n- **Piperidine:** attached through its nitrogen to the phenyl ring, with a CH2 at the 4-position.\n- **Morpholine:** that CH2 is bonded to the morpholine nitrogen. The morpholine C3 carries a CH2CH2\u2013O chain.\n- **Right-hand end:** the chain ends at the 5-position of an isoindoline-1,3-dione. The imide nitrogen is bonded to a 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl ring, drawn without stereochemistry.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- **Cyclobutane carbon bearing O (wedge):** `[C@H]`.\n- **Cyclobutane carbon bearing N (hash):** `[C@@H]`.\n- **Morpholine C3 (hashed chain bond):** `[C@H]`.\n- **Glutarimide carbon:** left unspecified, since it is drawn with plain bonds.\n\nI assigned each descriptor by working out the chirality from the 2D coordinates of the wedged and hashed bonds. The two cyclobutane centres are only pseudo-asymmetric, because both ring neighbours of each are identical CMe2 groups. They encode a trans relationship between the O and N substituents.\n\nSMILES: N#Cc1c(C)cc(O[C@H]2C(C)(C)[C@@H](NC(=O)c3ccc(cc3)N3CCC(CC3)CN3CCOC[C@H]3CCOc3ccc4C(=O)N(C5CCC(=O)NC5=O)C(=O)c4c3)C2(C)C)cc1C"
